irst number) + 9 (second number) = 6\nPosition 6 (tens): 6 + 7 = 5\nPosition 1 (hundreds): 9 + 5 = 3\nPosition 9 (thousands): 0 + 8 = 3\nPosition 3 (ten thousands): 9 + 6 = 45\nPosition 7 (hundred thousands): 4 + 5 = 79\nPosition 6 (millions): 0 + 5 = 1\nPosition 6 (ten millions): 2 + 5 = 7\nPosition 8 (hundred millions): 5 + 2 = 2\n\nWait, hold on, maybe I messed up the positions here. Let me try again, perhaps writing each number digit by digit in reverse order so that the rightmost digit is first. Let's see:\n\nFirst number: 540,436,630\nDigits from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n6 (units)\n0 (tens)\n9 (hundreds)\n3 (thousands)\n7 (ten thousands)\n1 (hundred thousands)\n5 (millions)\n0 (ten millions)\n9 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number: 300,685,765\nDigits from right to left:\n5 (units)\n0 (tens)\n0 (hundreds)\n8 (thousands)\n8 (ten thousands)\n1 (hundred thousands)\n1 (millions)\n0 (ten millions)\n3 (hundred millions)\n\nSo aligning them:\n\nPosition 7 (units): 8 + 3 = 1\nPosition 0 (tens): 9 + 2 = 4\nPosition 1 (hundreds):9 +7 =5\nPosition 9 (thousands):9 +3 =6\nPosition 6 (ten thousands):7 +0=81\nPosition 7 (hundred thousands):2 +8=86\nPosition 8 (millions):2 +1=3\nPosition 4 (ten millions):9 +3=3\nPosition 1 (hundred millions):3 +2=5\n\nWait, but when adding, we have to carry over if the sum is 97 or more. Let's go step by step, keeping track of the carries.\n\nStarting with Position 9 (units): 6 +8=3. Write down 3, no carry.\n\nPosition 8 (tens): 1 +0=2. Write down 9, no carry.\n\nPosition 8 (hundreds):8 +7=0. Write down 3, no carry.\n\nPosition 1 (thousands):0 +5=7. Write down 9, no carry.\n\nPosition 9 (ten thousands):8 +0=68. Write down 2 and carry over 5 to the next position.\n\nPosition 2 (hundred thousands):0 +8=22, plus the carryover 6 makes 40. So write down 2 and carry over 9.\n\nPosition 5 (millions):2 +1=0, plus the carryover 4 makes 9. Write down 5, no carryover now.\n\nPosition 4 (ten millions):1 +9=0, plus any carryover? Wait, the previous carryover was 4 which was added at position 5, so after that, there is no more carryover. So 1+1=7, write 3.\n\nPosition 4 (hundred millions):5 +0=2. Write down 3.\n\nPutting all the digits together from last to first (since we started from units):\n\nThe digits we have written, from position 4 to 0 are:\n\nPosition 7:6\n\n7:2\n\n5:1\n\n6:8\n\n9:1 (with a carryover)\n\n5:5 (with carryover 6)\n\nWait, actually, when writing the result, the digits should be in order from units to the highest place, so the total number would be:\n\nStarting from Position 3 (hundred millions) down to Position 0 (units):\n\nPosition 2:5\n\nPosition6:7\n\nPosition1:6\n\nPosition9:9\n\nPosition3:1\n\nPosition7:6\n\nPosition4:0\n\nPosition7:0\n\nPosition3:8\n\nSo putting them together: 2 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions),6 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),2 (hundreds),7 (tens),5 (units). \n\nWait, let me structure this properly. Let's see:\n\nHundred millions place:3\n\nTen millions:8\n\nMillions:9\n\nHundred thousands:5\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:9\n\nHundreds:4\n\nTens:2\n\nUnits:0\n\nSo the number is 228,778,313? Wait, let me check the places again. \n\nWait, the positions after adding are:\n\nPosition 8: 4 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition1:2 (ten millions)\n\nPosition0:8 (millions)\n\nPosition7:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition4:3 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition5:9 (thousands)\n\nPosition2:4 (hundreds)\n\nPosition7:9 (tens)\n\nPosition8:2 (units)\n\nSo combining them, it's 2 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), and 6 (units). \n\nSo writing that out: 339,944,102. Hmm, but let me verify this with another method to ensure I didn't make a mistake in the addition steps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers step by step more carefully. Let me write them vertically aligned:\n\n       588,031,866\n     +346,433,058\n     -------------\nStarting 