I should do this more systematically. Let me write down each digit position step by step:\n\nFirst number: 631,379,944\nBreaking it down digit by digit from right to left:\nPosition 2 (units): 1\n3 (tens): 0\n0 (hundreds): 4\n1 (thousands): 1\n3 (ten thousands): 6\n1 (hundred thousands): 1\n1 (millions): 6\n5 (ten millions): 6\n1 (hundred millions): 2\n76 (billions): 0\n\nWait, actually, let me count the digits properly. The number 201,949,887 has 7 digits. Wait, 7 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 3 (tens?), no wait, maybe I'm getting confused. Let me parse it correctly.\n\nWait, 639,403,926:\n\nStarting from the left:\nDigit 0: 1 (hundred millions place? Wait, commas separate every three digits. So 548,667,760 is 7 (hundred millions?), 5 (ten millions?), 9 (millions?), then 6 (hundred thousands?), 3 (ten thousands?), 0 (thousands?), 6 (hundreds?), 2 (tens?), 3 (units?).\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write each number with commas to see:\n\nFirst number: 519,090,961. Let's separate each digit:\n\nBillions place: 5 (since the first comma is after three digits from the right, so 0 is in the hundred millions place actually. Wait, maybe I need to recall the place values properly.\n\nWait, let's take 714,032,845:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nThe number is 2,226,947,47? Wait no, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 145,930,466 is actually:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 6\n\nTens:2\n\nHundreds:4\n\nThousands:3\n\nTen thousands:4\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nMillions:4\n\nTen millions:5\n\nHundred millions:3\n\nBillions: ?\n\nWait, 661,459,521 has 8 digits. Let me count:\n\nDigit 5 (rightmost): 3 (units)\n\n8: 4 (tens)\n\n7: 8 (hundreds)\n\n2:2 (thousands)\n\n2:3 (ten thousands)\n\n1:1 (hundred thousands)\n\n6:8 (millions)\n\n3:1 (ten millions)\n\n2:1 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, so the highest place here is hundred millions (the 0th digit from the right), so it's 4 hundred million, 2 ten millions, 5 millions, 2 hundred thousands, 4 ten thousands, 5 thousands, 7 hundred, 0 tens, and 3 units. So the total is 519,092,324.\n\nThe second number is 271,314,120. Let's break it down similarly:\n\nDigits from right:\n\n2:8 (units)\n\n0:7 (tens)\n\n5:3 (hundreds)\n\n8:9 (thousands)\n\n6:0 (ten thousands)\n\n3:7 (hundred thousands)\n\n8:8 (millions)\n\n0:6 (ten millions)\n\n1:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSo the second number is 0 hundred million, 7 ten millions, 9 millions, 0 hundred thousands, 8 ten thousands, 0 thousand, 6 hundreds, 7 tens, 3 units.\n\nNow, adding them digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n2st digit (units): 2 + 8 = 06. Write 9, carryover 4.\n\n1nd digit (tens):9 +0 =48, plus carryover 9 is 57. Write 2, carryover6.\n\n4rd digit (hundreds):4 +6 =4, plus 5 is 3. Write 3, no carryover.\n\n2th digit (thousands):9 +9 =5. Write 3, no carryover.\n\nWait, hold on, first number's thousands digit is 0, second's thousands digit is 7. So 2+9=2. Okay, that's right.\n\n2th digit (ten thousands):3 +4 =7. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nWait, first number's ten thousands digit is 7, second's is 1. 4+4=6, yes.\n\n0th digit (hundred thousands):6 +4 =8. Write 6, no carryover.\n\n9th digit (millions):2 +5 =26. Write 2, carryover3.\n\nWait, first number's millions digit is 3, second's is2. 8+8=46, so write 1, carryover9.\n\n3th digit (ten millions):8 +6 =75, plus carryover5=90. Write1, carryover9.\n\n1th digit (hundred millions):9 +9=88, plus carryover9=43. Write9, carryover5.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carryover6 becomes the next digit (billions place). So total is 0 followed by the digits we have.\n\nPutting all together from right to left:\n\nDigits collected:\n\n2st:5\n\n4nd:7\n\n7rd:7\n\n2th:7\n\n4th:5\n\n4th:0\n\n0th:1\n\n3th:8\n\n8th:8\n\nPlus the final carryover9 at the 28th digit (billions place).\n\nSo the sum is 7,5 followed by the rest. Let's arrange them properly:\n\nStarting from the highest digit:\n\nBillions place:7\n\nHundred millions:9\n\nTen millions:3\n\nMillions:6\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nThousands:8\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:6\n\nUnits:7\n\nWait, let's structure the digits properly in order:\n\nThe digits from the addition (from right to left):\n\nPosition 7:8\n\n0:6\n\n1:4\n\n8:6\n\n6:6\n\n3:5\n\n9:6\n\n2:9\n\n2:9\n\n44:3\n\nTherefore, writing from left to right (billions down to units):\n\n6 (billions), 2 (hundred millions),3 (ten millions),4 (millions),2 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),3 (hundreds),2 (tens),2 (units). So the number is 2,801,705,843?\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nBreaking down the digits:\n\nBillions digit:7\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:9\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:6\n\nTens:8\n\nUnits:5\n\nSo combining them: 5,039,718,774?\n\nWait, let me verify that:\n\n3,981,175,499\n\nYes, because:\n\n8,484,647,655\n\nplus 352,664,167 would be 4,254,851,488.\n\nWait, but let me confirm each part:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:5\n\nTens:9 → so 03\n\nHundreds:1 → 531\n\nThousands:5 → 7,486 → total so far up to thousands: 3,971\n\nTen thousands:2 → 46,987 → total up to ten thousands: 06,807\n\nHundred thousands:3 → 869,112 → total to hundred thousands: 752,059\n\nMillions:5 → 2,819,428 → total to millions: 5,122,848\n\nTen millions:4 → 24,771,895 → total to ten millions: 20,638,149\n\nHundred millions:9 → 049,191,983 → total to hundred millions: 405,336,402\n\nBillions:6 → 0,971,692,465 → total: 2,704,163,393.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n\nWait, but let me cross-verify with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the addition steps earlier. Let me try adding again more carefully.\n\nFirst number: 915,107,031\n\nSecond number:169,825,847\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n      383,140,995\n\n    +261,528,401\n\n    --------------\n\nStarting from th