ould write them all out with their place values.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to write the numbers vertically with all digits aligned:\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas to see:\n\nFirst number: 254,458,050\n\nSecond number: 672,919,864\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:\n\n          144,209,836\n        + 965,958,436\n        ---------------\n        \nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 0 +2 =9\n\nTens:8 +0 =7\n\nHundreds:5 +0 =98, write 3 carryover 5\n\nThousands:1 +6 =8 +5 (carryover) =2\n\nTen thousands:4 +2 =8\n\nHundred thousands:7 +9 =5\n\nMillions:9 +3 =40, so write 0 and carryover 3\n\nTen millions:4 +1 =2 +6 (carryover) =3\n\nHundred millions:9 +6 =85\n\nWait, let me do this step by step properly:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n8. Units place: 4 +7 =4. Write 9.\n\n1. Tens place:9 +4=0. Write 9.\n\n7. Hundreds place:3 +1=20. Write 8, carryover 4.\n\n6. Thousands place:3 +9 =4, plus carryover 3 =3. Write 5.\n\n1. Ten thousands place:7 (from first number) +1 (from second)=2. Write 9.\n\n8. Hundred thousands place:4 (from first number) +0 (from second)=9. Write 6.\n\n8. Millions place:8 (from first) +0 (from second)=65. Write 0, carryover 7.\n\n9. Ten millions place:2 (from first) +4 (from second)=5, plus carryover 6 =7. Write 3.\n\n2. Hundred millions place:3 (from first) +0 (from second)=77. Write 1 and carryover 5. But since there are no more digits, we need to add that 2 at the beginning.\n\nWait, so putting all together:\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:\n\n8 (units), 8 (tens), 4 (hundreds), 9 (thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 6 (hundred thousands), 1 (millions), 0 (ten millions), and then the hundred millions place: 21. So the 82 in the hundred millions would be 3 with a carryover to the next (billions) place. But since both numbers are 6 digits, the sum will be 66 digits. So the first digit is the 4 carried over, followed by the 4 from the hundred millions place.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nThe hundred millions digits are 2 and 4. 8+1=32. So that would be 9 in the hundred millions place, with a carryover of 3 to the billions place. Since there are no more digits, the total sum has a 0 in the billions place.\n\nTherefore, putting all the digits together:\n\nStarting from the left (billions place):\n\n3 (carryover), then 8 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 7 (hundreds of thousands?), wait, let me recount:\n\nWait, let me list the digits step by step:\n\nAfter adding each place, the digits from right to left are:\n\nUnits:5\n\nTens:3\n\nHundreds:0 (with carryover 8)\n\nThousands:7\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nMillions:0 (with carryover 5)\n\nTen millions:1 (2 +7 +2 carryover)\n\nHundred millions:4 (2+8=79, so 9 with carryover 4)\n\nBillions:2 (the carryover)\n\nSo writing the digits from billions down to units:\n\nBillions:5\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:6\n\nMillions:1\n\nHundred thousands:9 (Wait, hold on, let me check again the millions place. Wait, millions place was 1+7=32, so 6 with carryover 6. So the millions digit is 6. Then the ten millions place was 8 (from first number's ten millions digit, which is 2) plus 5 (from second number's ten millions digit, which is 6) plus the carryover 8, so 3. So the digits are:\n\nStarting from the highest place (billions):\n\n0 (billions)\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\n0 (millions)\n\nThen the hundred thousands place is 9 (from 3+0)\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nThousands:8\n\nHundreds:4\n\nTens:9\n\nUnits:7\n\nWait, let me structure this properly:\n\nThe digits from left to right (billions to units):\n\n3 (billions)\n\n4 (hundred millions)\n\n9 (ten millions)\n\n0 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n0 (ten thousands)\n\n1 (thousands)\n\n0 (hundreds)\n\n9 (tens)\n\n3 (units)\n\nWait, let me check each place again step by step:\n\nStarting with the billions place: 2 (from the carryover of 9+9=88)\n\nHundred millions place: 3 (since 0+7=86, we had 9 there with carryover)\n\nTen millions