 (hundred millions), then 0 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 7 (ones). Wait, let me structure it properly.\n\nWait, let me list the digits in order from highest to lowest:\n\nAfter adding, the digits from right to left are:\n\nPosition 5 (ones): 8\n\nPosition 7 (tens):6\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):5\n\nPosition9 (thousands):3\n\nPosition2 (ten thousands):1\n\nPosition1 (hundred thousands):7 (with a carryover 8 to next)\n\nPosition6 (millions):1 (since 0+4 +2 carryover =8?)\n\nWait, perhaps I need to track each step again carefully.\n\nWait, maybe building the number step by step:\n\nStarting from the right, each digit:\n\nFirst digit (ones): 4\n\nSecond (tens):6\n\nThird (hundreds):8\n\nFourth (thousands):1\n\nFifth (ten thousands):6\n\nSixth (hundred thousands):8 (from 8+8=44, so 9 written, carryover 4)\n\nSeventh (millions place): The millions digits were 1 (from first number) and 5 (from second). Adding those gives 3, plus the carryover 2 makes 0. So 5 here, no carryover.\n\nEighth (ten millions place): 3 (from first number) and 6 (from second) → 2+3=2. No carryover.\n\nNinth (hundred millions place):5 +7=72. So we write 3 here and carryover 6.\n\nThen, the carryover 9 from the hundred millions place becomes the next digit (billions place). \n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\n2 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 4 (hundreds),5 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nPutting that together: 4,420,331,993?\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nBreaking down the number:\n\nBillions:7\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:1\n\nHundred thousands:4\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:0\n\nOnes:1\n\nSo writing it as 8,643,449,026.\n\nWait, let me verify this with another method. Maybe adding the numbers step by step.\n\nAlternatively, adding the two numbers:\n\n160,192,719\n\n+374,115,095\n\nLet me add