s digit: 7\n\nSecond number's hundreds digit: 5\n\n8 + 3 = 93, plus 3 is 58. Write 6, carryover 8. Hundreds digit is 4.\n\nThousands place:\n\nFirst number's thousands digit: 7\n\nSecond number's thousands digit: 7\n\n0 + 4 = 1, plus 0 is 0. Write 1, no carryover. Thousands digit is 3.\n\nTen thousands place:\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit: 3\n\nSecond number's ten thousands digit: 2\n\n3 + 5 = 6. Write 4. No carryover. Ten thousands digit is 6.\n\nHundred thousands place:\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit: 5\n\nSecond number's hundred thousands digit: 0\n\n0 + 7 = 53. Write 6, carryover 5. Hundred thousands digit is 6, carryover 6.\n\nMillions place:\n\nFirst number's millions digit: 9\n\nSecond number's millions digit: 7\n\n6 + 5 = 8, plus the carried 3 is 1. Write 0. Millions digit is 5. No carryover.\n\nTen millions place:\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit: 5\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit: 6\n\n4 + 4 = 6. Write 7. Ten millions digit is 2.\n\nHundred millions place:\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit: 5\n\nSecond number's hundred millions digit: 2\n\n4 + 4 = 7. Write 2. Hundred millions digit is 0.\n\nPutting all together, the sum should be:\n\n4 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones). \n\nWait, hold on, let me structure that properly:\n\nStarting from the highest place:\n\nHundred millions: 6\n\nTen millions: 7\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nThousands:7\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:3\n\nWait, but when I put them together, it would be 289,761,832? Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the order of the digits. Let me list each digit position step by step:\n\nLet me write the result digit by digit:\n\n- Hundred millions place: 6 + 6 = 0\n\n- Ten millions place: 7 + 4 = 9\n\n- Millions place: 3 + 0 = 9, plus the carryover 9 from the hundred thousands place? Wait no, the carryover from the hundred thousands place was from the 77, so the hundred thousands place had a carryover of 7 to the millions place. Let me retrace:\n\nWait, when we did the hundred thousands place:\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is 3 (from 159,127,543: the digits are 4 2 3 1 9 5 4 0 1, so hundred thousands is the 0th digit from the left, which is 0). \n\nSecond number's hundred thousands digit is 7 (694,511,628: digits are 8 6 4 0 5 0 1 6 3; the 6th digit is 8). \n\nSo 4 +8 =58. So we write 0 in the hundred thousands place and carryover 8 to the millions place. \n\nThen the millions place is 3 (from first number) +3 (from second number) + carryover 6 = 1. So that's correct. \n\nSo the millions digit is 8. \n\nThen the ten millions place: first number's ten millions digit is 2, second's is 0, so 6+4=7. \n\nHundred millions: 1+2=4. \n\nSo putting all together, the digits from left to right are:\n\nHundred millions:8, ten millions:4, millions:1, hundred thousands:3, ten thousands:8, thousands:8, hundreds:0, tens:9, ones:1.\n\nSo the number is 189,782,699?\n\nWait, let's parse that:\n\n8 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions) => 275 million,\n\nthen 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands) => 668 thousand,\n\nand 7 (hundreds),3 (tens),2 (ones) => 857.\n\nSo altogether, 015,804,822.\n\nBut let me check with another method to be sure. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, adding 501,135,623 + 519,650,132.\n\nLet me see if I can break this down:\n\n121,059,972 + 076,815,588 = 782,578,914\n\nThen add 78,398,435:\n\n877,066,903 + 65,014,730.\n\nFirst add 39,393,369: 913,481,672\n\nThen add 8,561,062:\n\n907,837,768 + 7,370,043.\n\nLet's do 367,185,514 + 7,347,825 = 907,874,455\n\nThen add 732,303:\n\n948,864,754 + 430,203.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Maybe adding 432,953 first: 262,794,105 + 547,655 = 793,425,472\n\nThen add 92,184: 244,186,704 + 62,404\n\nAdding 34,449: 000,655,032\n\nThen 9,891: 335,120,236 +8,442 = 220,083,