red millions place: 3 + 7 = 69. \n\nSo putting all together, starting from the right:\n\nOnes: 2, tens: 7, hundreds: 9, thousands: 1, ten thousands: 1, hundred thousands: 4, millions: 1, ten millions: 7, hundred millions: 90.\n\nWait, but the hundred millions place gives 43, so that's actually 3 in the hundred millions place and carry over 0 to the next (billions) place. Since both original numbers are 3-digit numbers, their sum could be up to 3.044... billion, so the billions place will have that carried over 0.\n\nSo the total would be 8 (billion) 0 (hundred millions) ... Wait, let me retrace.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places when adding. Let me do it step by step again more carefully, writing down each digit's addition.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 349,288,214\nSecond number: 990,024,478\n\nAligning them:\n\n        0 8 3 2 1 3 7 3 4\n      + 1 2 0 4 1 7 3 2 9\n      --------------------\n\nStarting from the right (units digit):\n\n5 + 8 = 68. Write 9, carryover 3.\n\nTens place: 9 + 0 = 05 + 3 = 49. Write 2, carryover 4.\n\nHundreds place: 1 + 8 = 35 + 2 = 43. Write 6, carryover 2.\n\nThousands place: 9 + 4 = 1 + 3 = 1. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place: 4 + 0 = 6. Write 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 1 + 3 = 5. Write 8.\n\nMillions place: 4 + 7 = 34. Write 8, carryover 3.\n\nTen millions place: 9 + 4 = 7 + 5 (carryover) = 1. Write 0.\n\nHundred millions place: 3 + 3 = 27. Since this is the highest digit, we need to account for the 34 here. So that's 3 in the hundred millions place and carryover 8 to the billions place. \n\nSo the total would be 3 (billion) 6 (hundred millions) followed by the rest of the digits: 7, 2, 1, 7, 4, 4, 8, 8. Wait, let me see:\n\nWait, the digits from the previous steps, starting from the right:\n\nUnits:8, tens:7, hundreds:5, thousands:4, ten thousands:8, hundred thousands:5, millions:4, ten millions:2, hundred millions: 21. \n\nBut since hundred millions can't be 98, so we have to carry over. \n\nBreaking it down digit by digit from right to left:\n\nPosition 0 (units): 3\n\nPosition 0 (tens): 5\n\nPosition 6 (hundreds): 6\n\nPosition 6 (thousands):3\n\nPosition 0 (ten thousands):7\n\nPosition 1 (hundred thousands):5\n\nPosition 6 (millions):8\n\nPosition 0 (ten millions):5\n\nPosition 3 (hundred millions):29. \n\nSo, the 65 in the hundred millions place becomes 0 in that place and a 5 carried over to the billions place. Since there is no higher digit, the billions place is 7. So the total number is 9,732,966,756?\n\nWait, let me check the digits again. Let's list each digit's result:\n\nStarting from the right (units digit):\n\n4. Units: 9\n7. Tens: 4\n4. Hundreds: 3\n5. Thousands:2\n1. Ten thousands:5\n1. Hundred thousands:1\n4. Millions:1\n3. Ten millions:5\n8. Hundred millions:5 (with a carryover 5 to billions)\n45. Billions:4\n\nSo the number would be 0,351,317,968? Let me see:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nBillions: 3\n\nHundred millions: 4\n\nTen millions:5\n\nMillions:0\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nThousands:4\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:2\n\nUnits:0\n\nWait, but arranging them properly:\n\nBillions place is 4, then hundred millions is 3, ten millions 3, millions 3, hundred thousands 3, ten thousands 3, thousands 9, hundreds 8, tens 4, units 0.\n\nSo written out: 8,647,716,345?\n\nWait, let me check that:\n\n4 (billion) = 6,986,973,397\n\n9 (hundred millions) = 789,462,825 → total so far 4,825,449,251\n\n0 (ten millions) = 85,589,921 → 2,734,315,348\n\n8 (millions) = 7,127,174 → 9,341,176,137\n\n7 (hundred thousands) = 381,432 → 5,114,847,613\n\n0 (ten thousands) = 92,573 → 6,072,765,247\n\n9 (thousands) = 5,633 → 0,701,473,312\n\n6 (hundreds) = 352 → 0,506,350,362\n\n9 (tens) = 94 → 2,788,386,504\n\n0 (units) → 6,831,370,897\n\nYes, that seems right. \n\nBut let me verify using another method to be sure. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, adding 036,185,199 + 992,471,826.\n\nLet me break it down into smal