r from the total, I should get the first number.\n\nTotal is 548,401,995 minus 866,383,135.\n\nLet me compute that:\n\n005,320,752\n\n-702,505,039\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1-1=3\n\n4-5. Can't do, borrow. 7 becomes 22, borrow from the next digit. But the next digit is 6, so need to keep borrowing.\n\nHmm, this might get complicated, but let's try:\n\nStarting from units place:\n\n2 -2 =2.\n\nTens place: 1 -7. Since 0 <7, need to borrow.\n\nThe hundreds place is 9, so we have to go to thousands place which is 4.\n\nSo borrow 2 from thousands place (8 becomes 2), then the hundreds place becomes 78, but we need to borrow for the tens place:\n\nSo the hundreds place (now 33) gives 2 to the tens place, becoming 8, and the tens place becomes 28.\n\nSo 07 -3 =7.\n\nHundreds place is now 2 (after giving 3 to tens), but originally it was 8, so after borrowing for thousands, it was 69, then gave 2 to tens, so 1.\n\nNow hundreds digit: 3 -3 (since the second number's hundreds digit is 7). Wait, actually, in subtraction, we need to consider the second number's digits.\n\nWait, perhaps this is getting too convoluted. Alternatively, maybe I can trust the previous addition steps since both methods gave the same result. Alternatively, perhaps I can compute 241,095,214 -523,318,017.\n\nLet me compute:\n\n995,547,743 minus 796,091,801.\n\nSubtract digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 2-3=5\n\nTens: 7-6. Can't do, so borrow 4 from hundreds.\n\nBut hundreds is 8, so need to go to thousands.\n\nThousands place is 1, so borrow 6, thousands becomes 8, then hundreds becomes 93.\n\nThen borrow 6 from hundreds (now 85 becomes 3), so tens becomes 34.\n\n77-4=5.\n\nHundreds: 5 (after borrowing) minus 9 (from second number's hundreds digit) =3.\n\nThousands: 3 (after borrowing) minus 5 (second number's thousands digit is 8). Wait, 4-7 can't do. So need to borrow from ten thousands.\n\nTen thousands place is 6. Borrow 8, ten thousands becomes 1, thousands bec