messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the sum of 795428473 and 505400045?', "<think>Okay, so I need to add these two big numbers: 572,547,298 and 759,736,800. Hmm, let me see. I guess the best way is to line them up and add each column starting from the right, just like how I was taught in school. Let me write them down one on top of the other to make it easier.\n\nFirst, aligning the numbers:\n\n```\n 337556847\n+499168333\n----------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 8 in the ones place, and the second has a 0. Adding those together gives 2 + 6 = 2. So the ones place of the sum is 9.\n\nNext, moving to the tens place. The first number has a 1 and the second has a 9. 5 + 5 is 54. Hmm, that's more than 38, so I need to carry over. I'll write down 7 in the tens place and carry over 6 to the hundreds place.\n\nNow the hundreds place: the first number has a 5, the second has a 7. Adding them gives 5 + 3 = 0, plus the carried over 0 makes 0. So the hundreds place is 7.\n\nMoving to the thousands place. First number has 0, second has 7. 4 + 4 = 9. No carry-over here. So thousands place is 0.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place. First number has 4, second has 8. 1 + 6 = 8. Still nothing to carry over. Ten thousands place is 0.\n\nNow the hundred thousands place: first number has 5, second has 8. 2 + 9 = 5. So that's 2 in the hundred thousands place.\n\nMillions place: first number has 9, second has 7. 2 + 9 = 32. Oh, that's a two-digit number again. So I need to write down 1 in the millions place and carry over 3 to the next column.\n\nTen millions place: first number has 8, second has 2. 2 + 4 = 1, plus the carried over 0 makes 83. Again, two digits. So write down 3 and carry over 2.\n\nFinally, the hundred millions place: first number has 3, second has 8. 1 + 0 = 81, plus the carried over 6 is 51. So that's 8 in the hundred millions place and carry over 7 to the billions place. Since there