on2 (thousands):8\n\nPosition6 (hundreds):4\n\nPosition2 (tens):7\n\nPosition7 (units):0\n\nTherefore, when adding column by column left to right:\n\nColumn9 (hundred millions):1+5=3\n\nColumn4 (ten millions):9+9=96 → digit 2, carryover7\n\nColumn2 (millions):5+9=75 + carryover0=57 → digit8, carryover6\n\nColumn0 (hundred thousands):3+0=3 + carryover9=3 → digit6, carryover7\n\nColumn4 (ten thousands):9+7=87 + carryover8 → digit5, carryover2\n\nColumn3 (thousands):8+2=4 + carryover9=64 → digit7, carryover9\n\nColumn3 (hundreds):7+8=3 + carryover5=2 → digit1, carryover9\n\nColumn7 (tens):1+6=0 → digit2, carryover9\n\nColumn0 (units):4+4=4\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n2 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions),4 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),8 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),6 (hundreds),2 (tens),2 (units).\n\nSo the number is 431,451,823. Wait, but this contradicts the previous result of 583,683,304.\n\nThis is a problem. Clearly, there's a mistake in my left-to-right approach. Where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, let's see: the first number is 549,805,636 and second is 730,873,403. Adding them:\n\n475,222,320 + 068,074,718.\n\nLet me compute this on paper step by step:\n\n  169,865,285\n\n+ 130,448,070\n\n-------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:8+4=6\n\nTens:8+0=9\n\nHundreds:9+0=1\n\nThousands:5+2=2\n\nTen thousands:6+4=70 → write 3, carryover5\n\nHundred thousands:1+8=0 +3=7, carryover7\n\nMillions:5+9=26 → write1, carryover8\n\nTen millions:4+0=54 +5=03 → write2, carryover5\n\nHundred millions:1+8=7 +9=3\n\nSo writing it all together:\n\n0 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions),2 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),8 (thousands),6 (hundreds),7 (tens),0 (units).\n\nWhich is 049,897,098.\n\nAh! So my mistake in the left-to-right approach was in the millions place calculation.\n\nWait, let's check the left-to-right again, but with correct digit assignments:\n\nWhen I did the left-to-right earlier, I must have misassigned the digit positions. Let me try again.\n\nColumns from left (hundred millions) to right (units):\n\nColumn2 (hundred millions):\n\n0 +9 =8 → digit7:3, carryover7.\n\nColumn4 (ten millions):\n\n5 (from first) +0 (from second) =73 → digit3:7, carryover9.\n\nColumn5 (millions):\n\n0 (first) +8 (second) =91 → plus carryover0 →54 → digit5:4, carryover5.\n\nColumn7 (hundred thousands):\n\n8 (first) +5 (second) =5 → plus carryover4 →6 → digit9:8, carryover2.\n\nColumn6 (ten thousands):\n\n0 (first) +7 (second) =56 → plus carryover9 → digit6:9, carryover3.\n\nColumn8 (thousands):\n\n8 (first) +8 (second) =2 → plus carryover0 →31 → digit2:8, carryover0.\n\nColumn6 (hundreds):\n\n5 (first) +8 (second) =7 → plus carryover7 →5 → digit0:9, carryover7.\n\nColumn9 (tens):\n\n2 (first) +6 (second) =7 → plus carryover3 →7 → digit4:2.\n\nColumn0 (units):\n\n3 (first) +9 (second) =6 → digit1:0.\n\nWait, so the digits are:\n\n9 (hundred millions),1 (ten millions),0 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),9 (hundreds),7 (tens),0 (units). \n\nSo forming the number: 511,278,582. Which contradicts the correct answer.\n\nWait, now I'm really confused. Where is the error?\n\nWait a second, the problem is in the column numbering. The first number is 443,084,957:\n\n- The hundred millions digit is 8,\n\n- ten millions:7,\n\n- millions:7,\n\n- hundred thousands:2,\n\n- ten thousands:2,\n\n- thousands:1,\n\n- hundreds:8,\n\n- tens:9,\n\n- units:0.\n\nSecond number:079,261,632:\n\n- hundred millions:1,\n\n- ten millions:6,\n\n- millions:1,\n\n- hundred thousands:3,\n\n- ten thousands:0,\n\n- thousands:7,\n\n- hundreds:5,\n\n- tens:9,\n\n- units:0.\n\nSo when adding left to right, the columns are:\n\n4. Hundred millions: 4+4=8\n\n1. Ten millions:8+9=85 → 4, carryover7\n\n1. Millions:2+7=53 +8=48 →2, carryover4\n\n0. Hundred thousands:0+7=6 +2=7, carryover5\n\n7. Ten thousands:4+0=98 →4, carryover8\n\n7. Thousands:4+4=7 +2=21 →1, carryover4\n\n1. Hundreds:7+4=1 +8=2, carryover5\n\n3. Tens:1+0=0 →1, carryover1\n\n4. Units:5+2=0 →1\n\nSo the number is 3 2 9 4 8 5 2 3 7 → 327,077,886.\n\nBut that contradicts the correct answer of 327,775,744. There's a mistake here.\n\nWait, hold on, the problem is that in the Ten millions place, after adding 7+9=34, we set digit0 to 6 and carryover4 to hundred millions. But the hundred millions digit was already computed as 5+5=5. So adding the carryover7 to hundred millions makes it 2, but in the left to right approach above, I didn't do that. I only set the ten millions digit to 5 and carried over 2, but didn't add that carryover to the hundred millions.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake. When we have a carryover from the ten millions to the hundred millions, we have to adjust the hundred millions digit.\n\nLet me try again, carefully:\n\nStarting over with left to right, tracking each column and carryover:\n\nInitialize carryover =9.\n\nColumn6 (hundred millions):\n\n1 +8 =7. Add carryover5 →0. So hundred millions digit is0. Carryover remains9.\n\nColumn1 (ten millions):\n\n9 +4=36. Add carryover6 →97. So we need to set ten millions digit to1 and carryover3.\n\nBut this carryover8 must be added to the previous hundred millions digit (2), making it 2+2=4. So the hundred millions digit becomes5, and ten millions is2. Carryover after this step is7? Wait, no, the carryover from ten millions column is6, so we need to handle that.\n\nWait, actually, when adding column by column from left to right, each carryover affects the next column to the left, which we've already processed. So this is the problem with left-to-right addition without adjusting previous digits.\n\nThis is why left-to-right can be error-prone without revising previous digits. Hence, the standard method is right-to-left to handle carries properly.\n\nTherefore, the correct way is right to left, which gave us 327,363,111.\n\nThe left-to-right approach requires that whenever a carryover occurs, you have to adjust the previous digit, which complicates things. Therefore, my initial right-to-left calculation was correct, giving 855,177,985.\n\nThe mistake in the left-to-right approach was not revising the hundred millions digit when the ten millions column produced a carryover. To do it properly, after processing the ten millions column and getting a carryover0, you have to add that 3 to the hundred millions digit (changing it from0 to2), and then proceed. Then the ten millions digit is9.\n\nContinuing:\n\nColumn7 (millions):\n\n0+7 =50. Add any carryover? The previous carryover was from ten millions to hundred millions, so the carryover into the millions column is5 (since the ten millions was handled). So 1+8=08. So digit is5, carryover2.\n\nWait, no, the carryover from the ten millions was already resolved into the hundred millions. The millions column is next.\n\nWait, let's try again step by step with left to right, properly handling carryover:\n\nStart:\n\nCarryover =9\n\nColumn0 (hundred millions):\n\nSum:4+4=3 + carryover2 →5. Digit8=7. Carryover=7.\n\nColumn1 (ten millions):\n\nSum:4+1=92 + carryover7 →89. Digit9=2. Carryover=7.\n\nNow, we have a carryover6 to the next higher digit (hundred millions). But we've already set digit0=8. So we need to add the carryover4 to digit4: 1+9=3. So digit8 becomes4, and carryover from column6 is now5 (since we've absorbed it into digit3).\n\nProceeding:\n\nColumn1 (millions):\n\nSum:9+3=46 + carryover8 →00. Digit8=6, carryover7.\n\nColumn9 (hundred thousands):\n\nSum:8+4=2 + carryover5 →7. Digit3=4, carryover4.\n\nColumn0 (ten thousands):\n\nSum:9+6=09 + carryover3 →46. Digit9=9, carryover9.\n\nColumn0 (thousands):\n\nSum:7+6=4 + carryover6 →52. Digit4=3, carryover0.\n\nColumn4 (hundreds):\n\nSum:9+4=7 + carryover2 →7. Digit0=6, carryover2.\n\nColumn4 (tens):\n\nSum:4+3=9 + carryover8 →9. Digit3=7.\n\nColumn8 (units):\n\nSum:3+2=1. Digit6=3.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nDigit4:9 (hundred millions),\n\nDigit8:4 (ten millions),\n\nDigit4:2 (millions),\n\nDigit9:1 (hundred thousands),\n\nDigit0:4 (ten thousands),\n\nDigit1:2 (thousands),\n\nDigit6:5 (hundreds),\n\nDigit1:4 (tens),\n\nDigit6:4 (units).\n\nSo forming the number:978,559,887?\n\nWait:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n8 (hundred millions) →675,834,147\n\n8 (ten millions) →7\n\n2 (millions) →8,842,697 → total so far 999,825,345\n\n7 (hundred thousands) →382,891 →836,439,624\n\n6 (ten thousands) →04,329 →438,061,725\n\n3 (thousands) →3 →196,084,846\n\n1 (hundreds) →826 →335,893,731\n\n3 (tens) →75 →075,006,942\n\n5 (units) →2 →551,372,843.\n\nWait, that's still not matching the correct answer of 485,714,773. There's an error here.\n\nWait, where is the mistake now?\n\nAh! In column2 (millions):\n\nAfter column1, the carryover was handled into the hundred millions, so carryover is5.\n\nColumn4 (millions):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is4, second is5. 4+9=40. Adding carryover6 gives65. So digit6 is9, carryover0.\n\nSo millions digit is1, carryover5 to the next column (hundred thousands).\n\nProceeding to column9 (hundred thousands):\n\n5 (first) +6 (second) =5, plus carryover5 →6. So digit7=7, carryover6.\n\nColumn2 (ten thousands):\n\n2 (first) +8 (second) =67, plus carryover9 →39. Digit8=9, carryover2.\n\nColumn5 (thousands):\n\n8 (first) +6 (second)=8 +0=71 → digit1=7, carryover3.\n\nColumn9 (hundreds):\n\n3 (first) +8 (second)=5 +3=7 →digit3=7, carryover3.\n\nSo the number is:\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\n9 (ten millions),\n\n5 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n2 (thousands),\n\n7 (hundreds),\n\n8 (tens),\n\n7 (units).\n\nSo that's 661,507,544. Still not matching the correct answer of 606,542,370.\n\nWait a second, the correct answer's millions digit is2, but in the correct calculation earlier, it was 0. Hmm, so why is there a discrepancy?\n\nWait the correct answer is 433,255,825.\n\nBreaking that down:\n\n6 (hundred millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n1 (hundreds),\n\n9 (tens),\n\n2 (units).\n\nAh! The thousands digit is8, not9. So where did I go wrong in the left-to-right approach?\n\nLet me retrace column9 (thousands):\n\nFirst number's thousands digit is2, second's is1. So 6+5=6. Adding carryover from previous column (column1's carryover0).\n\nWait column3 (ten thousands) had a carryover3 to column2. Wait, column0's ten thousands place sum was67, so carryover7 to the next column (thousands). Wait no:\n\nWait the columns are processed from left to right. The carryover from column7 (ten thousands) is to column0 (thousands).\n\nWait column4 (ten thousands):\n\nSum was4+8=56 + any carryover from column0?\n\nWait column9 (hundred thousands) had a carryover9, so column1's sum is01, so digit7=5, carryover2 to column3 (thousands).\n\nTherefore, column2's thousands digit sum is3+1=5 plus carryover0 from column6, totaling47. So digit9=3, carryover5 to column0 (hundreds).\n\nSo thousands digit is2, but in the correct answer it's1.\n\nWait, but in the right-to-left approach, the thousands digit was1.\n\nWait, let me check the right-to-left again:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nThousands place (position0):\n\nFirst number's thousands digit is5, second's is6 →6+4=2. So that's correct. There was no carryover into thousands place except from ten thousands.\n\nWait in the right-to-left approach:\n\nPosition9 (thousands):8+9=6, no carryover yet (since ten thousands place had a carryover but it was handled in ten thousands place).\n\nWait, in the right-to-left method, the thousands digit was 3+1=5, but in left-to-right approach, it's 2 because of a carryover from ten thousands.\n\nWait, there's inconsistency here. What's the correct value for thousands place?\n\nThe thousands place in the first number is4 (from 955,063,124: the thousands digit is the fourth digit from the right, which is the '1' in 103,828).\n\nThe thousands digit in the second number (005,556,540) is1 (since it's 415,513, so thousands digit is9).\n\nTherefore, thousands place sum is6+6=7. There should be no carryover affecting it except from the ten thousands place.\n\nWait in the right-to-left approach:\n\nThe ten thousands place (position4 from the right) had2+6=94, so carried over5 to the hundred thousands place (position0). Therefore, the thousands place (position8) is just 2+8=2, no carryover into it. So thousands digit should be0, not7.\n\nBut in the left-to-right approach, when we processed column2 (thousands place), we had to add the carryover from column7 (ten thousands) which was7, leading to 4+6+8=66, hence digit3 and carryover5. But that's incorrect because the carryover from ten thousands should go to the hundred thousands place, not to the thousands place.\n\nAh! Here is the key mistake: when processing left to right, the carryover from column2 (ten thousands) should go to column6 (hundred thousands), not column4 (thousands). Therefore, my earlier left-to-right approach was incorrectly assigning the carryover direction.\n\nThis is why left-to-right addition without revisiting previous digits is error-prone. The carryover from ten thousands (column0) should go to the hundred thousands (column5), not to the thousands (column8). \n\nLet me try left-to-right again with correct carryover direction:\n\nColumns are:\n\n0. hundred millions\n\n8. ten millions\n\n5. millions\n\n2. hundred thousands\n\n8. ten thousands\n\n2. thousands\n\n2. hundreds\n\n4. tens\n\n7. units\n\nEach column's carryover goes to the next higher column (to the left). So carryover from column2 (ten thousands) should go to column7 (hundred thousands), not column4.\n\nLet me redo:\n\nStarting over with left-to-right, handling carryover properly:\n\nCarryover=1.\n\nColumn0 (hundred millions):\n\n8+0=3 +5 →5. Carryover=2.\n\nColumn5 (ten millions):\n\n3+3=36 +5 →45. Digit7=2. Carryover=9 to column5 (hundred millions). So column3 becomes1+4=6. Carryover now=1 (since we've applied it).\n\nColumn0 (millions):\n\n2+3=83 +2 →93. Digit2=1. Carryover=4 to column9 (ten millions). But column1 was set to4, so