), 1 (hundred millions),2 (ten millions),1 (millions),2 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),1 (hundreds),9 (tens),5 (units).\n\nWait, no. Wait, the positions:\n\nPosition7 is hundred millions: digit 4, but the carryover from position0 is 7 to billions.\n\nSo the digits in order from highest to lowest (billions down to units):\n\nBillions:4\n\nHundred millions:7\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:5\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:2\n\nTens:7\n\nUnits:9\n\nSo putting it together: 6,516,170,424.\n\nBut when I broke it into millions, I got 6,543,075,954. So why the discrepancy?\n\nAh, maybe I made a mistake in breaking into millions. Let's see:\n\nFirst number is 082,903,001. That's 144 million plus 665,705.\n\nSecond number is 408,988,379, which is 140 million plus 496,850.\n\nAdding the millions: 509 + 030 = 7,865 million. That's correct.\n\nAdding the smaller parts: 144,075 + 691,468.\n\nWait let's compute that again:\n\n352,997 + 021,775:\n\n993,983 + 436,279 = 4,600,904\n\nThen adding 70,188:\n\n3,424,124 + 11,331 = 3,952,397\n\nThen +9,408: 7,835,969 + 2,473 = 4,077,597. That's correct.\n\nSo total is 0,537,925,545 + 3,205,115 = 8,871,767,399.\n\nHmm. So there's a conflict between the two methods. Which one is right?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made an error in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nLet me check the digit-by-digit again step by step.\n\nFirst number: 402,398,082\n\nLet me write each digit position:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 7):\n\n7:1\n\n0:2\n\n8:5\n\n6:7\n\n3:2\n\n1:9\n\n4:9\n\n9:4\n\n5:3\n\nSecond number:951,767,440\n\nDigits:\n\n6:6\n\n4:6\n\n9:3\n\n3:8\n\n0:9\n\n1:1\n\n1:8\n\n9:9\n\n5:6\n\nWait, hold on, perhaps I messed up the digit positions here. Let me write both numbers with their digits clearly:\n\nFirst number (764,246,788):\n\nDigit 6 (units):2\n\nDigit 0 (tens):7\n\nDigit5 (hundreds):3\n\nDigit1 (thousands):4\n\nDigit8 (ten thousands):5\n\nDigit8 (hundred thousands):1\n\nDigit