2 8 9 0 2 7 0\n\nSubtrahend:3 9 5 0 7 8 2 6 8 4\n\nWait, actually, the digits are:\n\nMinuend (7,992,053,550):\n\nDigit8:1 (units)\n\nDigit7:4 (tens)\n\nDigit9:5 (hundreds)\n\nDigit1:5 (thousands)\n\nDigit8:0 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit7:8 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit3:9 (millions)\n\nDigit5:3 (ten millions)\n\nDigit0:2 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit81:8 (billions)\n\nSubtrahend (8,325,454,564):\n\nDigit5:6 (units)\n\nDigit6:8 (tens)\n\nDigit6:8 (hundreds)\n\nDigit1:1 (thousands)\n\nDigit3:9 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit0:2 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit6:5 (millions)\n\nDigit2:6 (ten millions)\n\nDigit2:3 (hundred millions)? Wait, no:\n\nWait, the subtrahend is 3,866,763,912, so:\n\nDigit0:8 (units)\n\nDigit7:1 (tens)\n\nDigit6:1 (hundreds)\n\nDigit3:2 (thousands)\n\nDigit1:8 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit2:7 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit0:2 (millions)\n\nDigit0:2 (ten millions)\n\nDigit6:8 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit79:2 (billions)\n\nSo now, subtraction digit by digit:\n\nStarting from Digit8 (units):\n\nDigit0:0-8=6\n\nDigit6:4-4=4\n\nDigit7:1-5=5\n\nDigit5:9 (minuend) -4 (subtrahend). Need to borrow.\n\nBorrow from Digit0 (ten thousands place of minuend, which is8 →8). So Digit8 becomes55. 07-3=1.\n\nDigit7:1 (after borrow) -1 (subtrahend's digit4 is4). Need to borrow.\n\nBorrow from Digit0 (hundred thousands place of minuend is4 →3). Digit0 becomes06. 87-0=9.\n\nDigit3:7 (after borrow) -1 (subtrahend's digit8 is4). Need to borrow.\n\nBorrow from Digit6 (millions place of minuend is8 →0). Digit3 becomes08. 39-8=6.\n\nDigit1:7 (after borrow) -4 (subtrahend's digit5 is5). Need to borrow.\n\nBorrow from Digit6 (ten millions place of minuend is1 →2). Digit3 becomes39. 81-6=7.\n\nDigit9:5 (after borrow) -9 (subtrahend's digit1 is0). 0-1=8.\n\nDigit0:1 (minuend's hundred millions) -8 (subtrahend's digit5 is9). Need to borrow.\n\nBorrow from Digit78 (billions place of minuend is8 →2). Digit0 becomes46. 76-9=5.\n\nDigit35:5 (after borrow) -1 (subtrahend's digit03) =3.\n\nSo the result is:\n\nDigit88:6\n\nDigit7:9\n\nDigit5:4\n\nDigit5:2\n\nDigit5:1\n\nDigit4:9\n\nDigit0:4\n\nDigit7:7\n\nDigit9:5\n\nDigit4:9\n\nThus, the result is 1 7 2 1 7 6 0 6 8 3 → 997,717,658. \n\nBut this contradicts the alternative method where it should be 517,877,476. \n\nWait, but in the alternative method I did:\n\n2,622,588,546 -119,547,121 =599,034,520\n\nThen subtract 56,121,010 from that:\n\n530,595,225 -02,164,761.\n\nLet me compute this:\n\n009,343,277\n\n-\xa0 18,033,105\n\n----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4-9=9\n\n5-5=6\n\n5-5=2\n\n9-3 → borrow.\n\nBorrow from ten thousands (8→7), thousands becomes59. 39-4=1.\n\nTen thousands:3 -4 → borrow.\n\nBorrow from hundred thousands (2→6), ten thousands becomes69. 98-5=5.\n\nHundred thousands:6 -8 → borrow.\n\nBorrow from millions (1→7), hundred thousands becomes49. 74-0=4.\n\nMillions:9 -3 → borrow.\n\nBorrow from ten millions (5→2), millions becomes59. 21-0=4.\n\nTen millions:0 -4=2.\n\nHundred millions:0 -0=6.\n\nSo the result is 352,307,932. \n\nThus, the discrepancy arises because in the direct subtraction I considered the subtrahend as 6,069,665,090, but actually the subtrahend is 065,195,982 which is 1,226,941,444. However, the result of the direct subtraction gave me 574 million instead of 424 million, but the alternative method gives the correct result. \n\nThis implies that there's a mistake in the digit positions during the direct subtraction. Let me re-express the subtrahend's digits correctly.\n\nWait, the subtrahend is 290,681,596. Breaking down its digits:\n\nDigit8 (units):9\n\nDigit3 (tens):2\n\nDigit6 (hundreds):5\n\nDigit1 (thousands):8\n\nDigit7 (ten thousands):9\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):6\n\nDigit1 (millions):2\n\nDigit1 (ten millions):8\n\nDigit8 (hundred millions):8\n\nDigit72 (billions):3\n\nSo when subtracting, the hundred millions digit of the subtrahend is0 (Digit8), not4 as I mistakenly thought earlier. \n\nWait, in the earlier direct subtraction I said Digit6 of subtrahend is1