umbers with commas to see:\n\nFirst number: 985,097,626\nSo commas after every three digits starting from the right. So that's 854 (million), 915 (thousand), 204.\n\nSecond number: 255,743,237\nSimilarly: 634 (million), 783 (thousand), 835.\n\nTherefore, when written out:\n\nFirst number: 2 3 8 3 9 7 3 7 4\nBut actually, in digits:\n\n0 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nWait, maybe I need to align them properly. Let me write both numbers digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 0 (042,938,874), 9 (21,998,821), 9 (3,318,621), 9 (965,231), 4 (06,789), 7 (5,417), 8 (087), 8 (73), 1 (7).\n\nSecond number: 7 (343,891,911), 1 (03,995,346), 3 (4,279,232), 6 (347,165), 5 (98,986), 5 (7,253), 8 (238), 6 (57), 5 (3).\n\nSo aligning them:\n\nHundred millions: 0 vs 4\n\nTen millions: 3 vs 5\n\nMillions: 8 vs 2\n\nHundred thousands: 2 vs 6\n\nTen thousands: 1 vs 5\n\nThousands: 2 vs 5\n\nHundreds: 9 vs 7\n\nTens: 5 vs 6\n\nOnes: 1 vs 2\n\nWait, so when adding, we start from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\nOnes place: 6 + 0 = 76. Write 6, carryover 4.\n\nTens place: 9 + 0 + 6 (carry) = 65. Write 4, carryover 9.\n\nHundreds place: 3 + 4 + 8 = 5. Wait, 5 (from first number's hundreds place) plus 6 (from second's hundreds place) is 1, plus the carryover 5 makes 0. So write 8, no carryover.\n\nWait, hold on, let me check again. Hundreds place:\n\nFirst number has 3, second has 7. 1 +4 =2, plus the carryover 9 from the previous step (which was 6 from the tens place addition). So 2 +0 =1. So yes, 4 with no carryover.\n\nMoving to thousands place: first number's thousands is 4, second's is 0. 4 +5 =50. Plus no carryover. So 65. So write 5, carryover 6.\n\nTen thousands place: first number has 0, second has 1. 1+0 =92. Plus the carryover 2 gives 05. So write 3, carryover 6.\n\nHundred thousands place: first has 5, second has 7. 7+5=58. Plus carryover 0 =24. So write 3, carryover 2.\n\nM