e ten thousands place. Let me check again:\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number's digits:\n\nBillions: 3 (since it's a 3-digit number, so up to hundred millions)\n\nHundred millions:5\n\nTen millions:6\n\nMillions:0\n\nHundred thousands:5\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:3\n\nUnits:1\n\nSecond number:\n\nBillions:3\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:6\n\nMillions:5\n\nHundred thousands:2\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:1\n\nUnits:9\n\nAdding each column:\n\nStarting from units:\n\n1+4=3\n\nTens:4+1=7\n\nHundreds:3+3=7\n\nThousands:9+2=9\n\nTen thousands:3+0=54 → write 3, carryover4\n\nHundred thousands:8+0=9 +2=0\n\nMillions:1+7=1\n\nTen millions:5+2=56 → write0, carryover5\n\nHundred millions:3+3=34 +5=57 → write8, carryover7\n\nBillions:1+7=2 +7=4\n\nSo the total is 9 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions),9 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),6 (hundreds),9 (tens),1 (units). So yes, that's 6,547,437,269.\n\nWait, but when I first thought I had 4,011,692,302, which is different. So I must have made a mistake in the first calculation. Let me see where.\n\nIn the first attempt, I thought:\n\nAfter the ten thousands place (03), then hundred thousands was 4+5 +5=6, but that was wrong. Wait, in the second number's hundred thousands place, it's actually 7, not 7. Because in the second number: 817,055,168. Breaking down:\n\n4 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions),0 (millions),3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands),1 (thousands), etc. So the hundred thousands digit is4, not 1. So in my first calculation I must have mistakenly taken the hundred thousands digit as 9, leading to an error. That was the mistake.\n\nSo the correct sum is indeed 0,319,597,874.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can add the numbers using another method, like splitting them into parts.\n\nLet me try adding 250,620,539 + 139,879,070.\n\nBreaking down both numbers:\n\nFirst number: 236,756,522 + 73,143,119 + 4,595,9