s place: 2 + 0 + 3 (carryover) = 5 (since the second number's hundreds digit is 0? Wait, hold on. Let me confirm the numbers again.\n\nWait, actually, let me write both numbers out digit by digit to ensure alignment.\n\nFirst number: 731,474,932\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nDigits (from right to left):\n\nPosition 3 (ones): 6\n\nPosition 4 (tens): 7\n\nPosition 8 (hundreds): 4\n\nPosition 1 (thousands): 4\n\nPosition 0 (ten thousands): 8\n\nPosition 7 (hundred thousands): 4\n\nPosition 5 (millions): 4\n\nPosition 5 (ten millions): 3\n\nPosition 6 (hundred millions): 4\n\nSecond number: 376,357,238\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nPosition 2 (ones): 5\n\nPosition 1 (tens): 8\n\nPosition 9 (hundreds): 5\n\nPosition 3 (thousands): 9\n\nPosition 7 (ten thousands): 3\n\nPosition 2 (hundred thousands): 9\n\nPosition 8 (millions): 8\n\nPosition 7 (ten millions): 7\n\nPosition 4 (hundred millions): 8\n\nPosition 36 (billions): 1 (since the first number doesn't have a billion, but the second does?)\n\nWait, actually, the second number is 212,909,043 which is 0 digits: 9 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait, maybe I messed up the breakdown.\n\nLet me write each number digit by digit with positions:\n\nFirst number: 457,320,072\n\nLet me parse it as:\n\nBillions: 6 (since it's 6 digits)\nHundred millions: 4\nTen millions: 5\nMillions: 1\nHundred thousands: 2\nTen thousands: 1\nThousands: 9\nHundreds: 4\nTens: 4\nOnes: 9\n\nWait, actually, commas are placed every three digits. So 884,790,853 is 7 2 8, 4 1 4, 3 3 2. So breaking down:\n\nDigit positions (starting from the right):\n\n0 (ones): 0\n\n0 (tens): 8\n\n9 (hundreds): 1\n\n8 (thousands): 3\n\n4 (ten thousands): 5\n\n2 (hundred thousands): 8\n\n9 (millions): 1\n\n4 (ten millions): 1\n\n8 (hundred millions): 4\n\nSo total 4 digits.\n\nSecond number: 581,155,044\n\nBreaking down similarly:\n\n6 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions