n thousands:8\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nMillions:1\n\nTen millions:7 (with carryover 2)\n\nHundred millions:0 (with carryover 3)\n\nThen the final carryover 6 becomes the billions place.\n\nSo the total is 6,960,075,237?\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number step by step.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nPosition 2 (ones): 7\n\nPosition 6 (tens): 7\n\nPosition 5 (hundreds): 2\n\nPosition 2 (thousands): 9\n\nPosition 6 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition 5 (hundred thousands):6\n\nPosition 2 (millions):1\n\nPosition 4 (ten millions):1\n\nPosition 1 (hundred millions):3\n\nPlus the carried over 4 in the billions place, making it 6 in the billions, so the total number is 2,937,574,741?\n\nWait, let me check again. Maybe I messed up the positions.\n\nWait, when we add the hundred millions place (digit 4):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 8, second is 2. 6 +2 =84, plus the carryover 4 from the ten millions place addition (which was 9+4 +5 carried over from millions? Wait, let me retrace.\n\nWait, let's go step by step with each digit's place:\n\nStarting from the right (digit 5 is the ones place):\n\nDigit 3 (Ones): 6 + 2 =4\n\nDigit 8 (Tens):0 +5=20. Write 1, carryover 6.\n\nDigit 0 (Hundreds):8 +7 +0=46. Write 4, carryover3.\n\nDigit8 (Thousands):4 +8=3 +8=9. Carryover7.\n\nDigit8 (Ten thousands):7 +0=4. Carryover4.\n\nDigit8 (Hundred thousands):3 +0=1. Carryover3.\n\nDigit9 (Millions):9 +7=4. Carryover1.\n\nDigit4 (Ten millions):8 +8=04. Write 7, carryover6.\n\nDigit1 (Hundred millions):4 +0=88 +4 (carryover) =23. So write 9, carryover5.\n\nNow, since we've reached the highest digit (hundred millions), the carryover6 becomes a new digit to the left, which is the billions place.\n\nSo the total is 5 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), then the rest.\n\nPutting all digits together from highest to lowest:\n\nBillions:8\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:2\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes